# Frontend (React)

## General

- Use React 19, TypeScript and Vite.
- Follow the existing project architecture and coding style.
- Prefer extending existing components over creating new ones.
- Keep changes minimal and focused.
- Never modify unrelated files.
- Do not introduce new dependencies unless explicitly requested.
- Do not duplicate code.

---

## Components

- Use functional components only.
- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility.
- Extract reusable UI into `src/components/ui`.
- Keep layout components inside `src/components/layout`.
- Keep page sections inside `src/components/sections`.
- Keep props strongly typed.
- Define local interfaces close to the component unless shared across multiple files.

---

## State Management

- Keep state as local as possible.
- Avoid unnecessary prop drilling.
- Do not introduce global state unless required.
- Avoid derived state when it can be computed.

---

## Performance

- Prioritize lightweight solutions.
- Use the minimum amount of JavaScript necessary.
- Avoid unnecessary React re-renders.
- Memoize components only when there is measurable benefit.
- Avoid premature optimization.
- Prefer CSS animations over JavaScript animations.
- Use lazy loading for large components or routes when appropriate.
- Keep the DOM structure simple.
- Avoid unnecessary wrappers.
- Remove unused imports and dead code.
- Avoid importing entire libraries when only individual modules are needed.
- Optimize images and SVG assets.
- Prefer SVG over bitmap graphics for icons and simple illustrations.
- Do not introduce heavy frontend libraries without explicit approval.

---

## Responsive Design

- Use a mobile-first approach.
- Support desktop, tablet and mobile layouts.
- Avoid horizontal scrolling.
- Prefer Flexbox and CSS Grid.
- Use responsive spacing and typography.
- Ensure components scale naturally across screen sizes.
- Test layouts at common breakpoints.

---

## UI / UX

- Prioritize readability and accessibility.
- Maintain consistent spacing.
- Maintain consistent typography.
- Maintain consistent color usage.
- Use clear visual hierarchy.
- Keep interactions intuitive.
- Prefer subtle animations.
- Avoid distracting visual effects.
- Keep interfaces modern, clean and uncluttered.

---

## Project Structure

- The SPA lives inside `frontend/`.
- `src/main.tsx` bootstraps the application.
- `src/App.tsx` composes the application.
- Static UI data belongs in `src/data` until it becomes backend-owned.
- Use `VITE_API_URL` for backend communication.
- Never hardcode API URLs.

---

## Styling

- Follow the existing styling approach.
- Keep styles close to components when appropriate.
- Avoid duplicated CSS.
- Prefer reusable utility classes where available.
- Keep CSS simple and maintainable.

---

## Testing & Validation

After frontend changes, run the smallest relevant validation first.

Prefer project scripts.

Typical validation order:

- `cd frontend && npm run lint`
- `cd frontend && npm run format:check`
- `cd frontend && npm test`
- `cd frontend && npm run build`

Docker / Make equivalents:

- `make frontend-lint`
- `make frontend-format`
- `make frontend-test`
- `make npm-build`

Prefer running only the checks affected by the current change before running the full validation suite.

---

## Testing

- Prefer Vitest.
- Prefer Testing Library.
- Keep tests close to the component (`*.test.ts` / `*.test.tsx`).
- Cover new logic with tests when appropriate.

---

## Before Finishing

Before considering the task complete:

- Ensure there are no obvious TypeScript errors.
- Ensure there are no lint errors.
- Ensure the project builds successfully.
- Ensure the implementation matches existing project conventions.
- Briefly summarize what changed.

## Visual Assets

- Reuse existing assets whenever possible.
- Prefer lightweight SVG assets.
- Avoid large background images unless necessary.
- Ensure decorative backgrounds never interfere with readability.
- Decorative assets must not negatively impact performance or responsiveness.